# Class Contains 1 Abstract Method and Must Therefore Be Declared Abstract or Implement the Remaining Methods

You encounter this fatal error when a child class fails to provide concrete implementations for all abstract methods defined in its parent. The specific message, `Class Validate contains 1 abstract method and must therefore be declared abstract or implement the remaining methods`, points directly to a structural gap in your inheritance hierarchy. In the provided source material, the `BaseValidator` class declares two abstract methods: `Validate` and `SetRange`. However, the child classes `Validate` and `setRange` split these responsibilities incorrectly.

The `Validate` class implements only the `Validate` method but ignores `SetRange`. Similarly, the `setRange` class implements only `SetRange` but leaves `Validate` undefined. Because neither child class fully satisfies the contract established by `BaseValidator`, PHP enforces the requirement at runtime. The error occurs because the language does not allow a non-abstract class to exist with unimplemented abstract signatures; it forces you to either declare the class as abstract itself or fill in every missing method.

## Why the Current Structure Fails

In object-oriented design, an abstract class serves as a blueprint that enforces specific behaviors that derived classes *must* provide. When `BaseValidator` declares two abstract functions, it signals that any concrete subclass must handle both validation logic and range setting logic.

Looking at the code for `class Validate`, you will see:

```php
class Validate extends BaseValidator
{
    public function Validate($string) { ... }
    // SetRange is missing here
}
```

Since `SetRange` is declared as `abstract` in the parent, it cannot be left empty or omitted in the child. The same logic applies to `class setRange`. By splitting the implementations across two separate classes that both extend the same abstract base, you create a situation where no single concrete instance can ever be instantiated without causing a fatal error.

The fix requires consolidating these responsibilities into a single concrete class or declaring the incomplete classes as abstract themselves if they are meant to serve as further templates.

## Correcting the Implementation

To resolve this error, you must ensure that any class extending `BaseValidator` implements both methods. Here is how you can refactor the `Validate` class to satisfy the parent contract:

```php
class Validate extends BaseValidator
{
    // Existing implementation for Validate
    public function Validate($string)
    {
        if (!preg_match('/[^A-Za-z]/', $string)) {
            echo "'{$string}' contains only alphabets!";
        } 
        if (is_numeric($string)) {
            echo "'{$string}' Conatins No. Only!<br>";
            echo '<br>';
        }
    }

    // Implementation for SetRange is now required
    public function SetRange($string)
    {
        if (!(strlen($string) > 4 && strlen($string) < 10)) {
            echo "You are not in range of 4-10";
        }
    }
}
```

Alternatively, if your intention was to have separate classes for different validation strategies, you should remove the `abstract` keyword from `BaseValidator` and make it a concrete class that provides default implementations, or declare both child classes as abstract and instantiate them only after further subclassing. However, given the context of creating a runnable application, merging the logic into one complete class is often the most pragmatic approach.

## Common Pitfalls in Abstract Class Design

A frequent mistake developers make is assuming that defining an abstract method in a parent class allows child classes to selectively implement only "core" behaviors while deferring others. This is not how PHP's type system works. If a method is marked `abstract`, it carries full weight as a mandatory requirement for every non-abstract descendant.

Another subtle issue often overlooked is the visibility and signature of methods. Even if you implemented a method with the same name, mismatched visibility (e.g., `protected` in parent vs `public` in child) or different parameter types can sometimes cause confusion, though PHP's error message specifically targets missing implementations in this context.

When designing validation logic, consider whether splitting logic into multiple classes extending a single abstract base is necessary. In many cases, a single `Validator` class with distinct methods for different checksâlike `ValidateFormat` and `SetRange`âis cleaner than forcing inheritance to handle method distribution. This approach avoids the overhead of multiple classes inheriting from an abstract template just to implement one method each.

If you are working within a Laravel ecosystem, similar patterns appear when defining custom gates or policies. While Laravel handles many validation rules internally, understanding how to correctly extend abstract contracts remains essential for building reusable, type-safe components. For more advanced validation strategies in modern PHP projects, exploring packages like `laravel/sanctum` or integrating with frameworks like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide examples of robust validation patterns that avoid these pitfalls entirely by leveraging built-in service contracts rather than manual abstract class inheritance.
